\section*{The isovector surface term in the Fayans functional}

\vspace*{-0.3em}

\subsection*{Discussion file MSU--FAU, status January
22. 2026}

\bigskip

This discussion file investigates the impact of the isovector surface
term, $\propto a_-^{(s)}(\nabla\rho_-)^2$, in the Fayans
functional. To that end, we performed fits with free variation of
$a_-^{(s)}\equiv$\texttt{asm} starting from the Fy(IVP3) functional
with finite pairing width 0.9 fm.  All results shown in the following
were produced for that newly fitted parametrization.



\begin{figure}[bth]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{test_isovector_surface.pdf}}
\caption{Quality measure $chi^2$ as function of the isovector-surface
parameter \texttt{asm}$\equiv a_-^{(s)}$.}
\label{fig:test_isovector_surface}
\end{figure}
The straightforward fit delivered
$a_-^{(s)}=0.005\pm 0.016$ well compatible with zero. To make sure
that we are not fooled by a rough $\chi^2$ landscape, we
counter-checked by a series of fits with fixed and systematically
varied \texttt{asm}.
%
Figure \ref{fig:test_isovector_surface} shows the resulting.  it
confirms perfectly the findings from the fit with free
$\texttt{asm}$. The isovector surface term does not improve much with
respect to the fit data. The same was found earlier in fit of Skyrme
forces where the isovector surface parameter is in most cases
compatible with zero within the $2\sigma$ band of variation. In case
of the Fayans Fy(IVP3) functional it is even less surprising. The
three isovector pairing parameters of Fy(IVP3) have already explored a
great deal of isovector freedom which, together with the finite-range
pairing, has delivered a huge gain in $\chi^2$.

\clearpage

\begin{figure}
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{alignmatrix-fit_asm.pdf}}
\caption{Matrix of CoD between model parameters and energies of
  $^{236}$U, namely ground state energy $E$, fission isomer energy
  $E^*$, and the isomer energy difference $\Delta E\equiv$DE for the
  fit of Fy(IVP3) including free isovector surface parameter
  \texttt{asm}$\equiv a_-^{(s)}$. The model parameters are denoted as
  they are named in the code.}
\label{fig:alignmatrix-fit_asm}
\end{figure}
Earlier papers argued that the isovector-surface term has a large
impact on the energy of the fission isomer in heavy elements. The new fit
opens the chance to investigate that in terms of correlation analysis
what we will do now. This gives us at the same time insight into the
correlations of the new parameter 'asm' with the other model
parameters. As a first overview, all correlations are shown in figure 
\ref{fig:alignmatrix-fit_asm} as matrix of coefficients of
determination (CoD). The CoD of the isomer energy 'DE(236U)' are
widely distributed over the model parameters without showing any clear
preference. This was already found in previous studies and the
introduction of the isovector-surface term does not change that. It is
rather the isovector-gradient term in the Fayans pairing which has a
surprisingly prevalence over the other parameters. The new parameter
'asm' correlates best with the isoscalar surface parameters.

%\clearpage

\begin{figure}
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{CoD_isovector_surface.pdf}}
\caption{Lower panel: The CoD between the isovector surface parameter
  \texttt{asm}$\equiv a_-^{(s)}$ and model parameters as well as
