Figure \ref{fig:CoD_isovector_surface} shows a cut of the CoD
 with
'asm' (lower panel) and the isomer energy in $^{236}$U (upper panel).
This allows to read off the correlations more quantitatively. It is
interesting to note that 'asm' has very little relation to pairing
properties. Quite different, the isomer energy is substantially
influenced by pairing, again a feature known from previous
studies. There is, indeed, some correlation with 'asm'. The question
is to what extend this is significant as 'asm' may be strongly
determined by the other model parameters. To see that more clearly
these inter-relation of model parameters, we will look also at the
multiple correlation coefficients (MCC).

%\clearpage

\begin{figure}
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{MCC_isovector_surface.pdf}}
\caption{Left panel: The MCC between the isovector surface parameter
  \texttt{asm}$\equiv a_-^{(s)}$ and groups of model parameters as
  indicated.  Right panel: The same for the MCC with the isomer energy
  in $^{236}$U, $^{238}$U, $^{240}$Pt, and $^{242}$Cm.}
\label{fig:MCC_isovector_surface}
\end{figure}
Figure \ref{fig:MCC_isovector_surface} shows the MCC of groups of
model parameters with the isovector-surface parameter 'asm' (left
panel) and the fission isomer energies in four actinides as indicated
(right panel). The MCC are very similar four all four actinides. They
show that 'asm' is already determined to 70\% by the isoscalar surface
term. Together with the spin-orbit term, we come already to 83\%.  The
isovector-surface term has very little freedom in the given Fayans
functional. The MCC with the isomer energy confirm that pairing as a
whole is the strongest influencer. Thus one should play with the
pairing parameters first if one aims to fine-tune the isomer.  The
impact of the surface term is not negligible either. Here we ought to
mention that we have checked the MCC two ways, first, only with the
isoscalar-surface parameter, and second, by also including 'asm'. The
difference is is about 0.01 in all four cases, i.e. negligible.

