\bigskip

The pure mean field results produce a deceivingly nice isomer energy,
but a too weak binding. For consistency, we must include the
rotational correction. This helps significantly for the binding
energy, but produces a too small isomer energy.  The under-binding of
heavy nuclei is a known disease of non-relativistic Skyrme mean-field
models and the Fayans functionals share this insufficiency. 
The energy of the fission isomer is particularly touchy. The
measurement yields the difference between ground state and isomer. But
both are heavily impacted by collective correlations (rotation and
vibration). The raw mean-field result is merely the difference of
minima. The rotational correction gives the energy difference between
angular momentum projected minima. The correlated energy is obtained
from subtracting the vibrational zero-point energy and adding the
collective energy of the ground state in the corresponding minimum. 
This can change the result by about 1 MeV. The actual number requires
a detailed calculation. Thus there  is still work ahead, at the
side of DFT functionals and at the side of collective calculations.


