\subsection*{Surface parameter and surface energy}

One has to distinguish the surface parameter of a model and the
surface energy. The surface parameter is the model parameter in front
of the gradient term $\propto(\nabla\rho_{0/1})^2$. The surface energy
is a model property of semi-infinite matter which depends on several
terms of the model, not only the gradient term. To explore the impact
of both these features, we show below the matrix of CoD between model
parameters and a couple of observables for SV-min. The surface
parameters of the model are 'b2' (isoscalar) and 'b2p'
(isovector). The surface energies are 'asurf' (isoscalar) and
'asurf-symm' (isovector). They are computed for semi-infinite matter
in semi-classical approximation (extended Thomas-Fermi) for which a
code for Skyrme functionals is readily available.

\bigskip


\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{alignmatrix-SVmin-surface}}

\bigskip

The surprise is that the surface parameters have little correlation
with the surface energies. They also have different CoD to the other
entries. The surface model parameters, b2 nd b2p, are strongly tied to
each other and, accordingly, they show the same weak correlations to
slope of symmetry energy $J$, isovector effective mass $\kappa$, and
isovector spin orbit b4p. There is also a faint correlation with the
fission isomer energy B(236U). 
The surface energies 'asurf' and 'asurf-symm' are more independent form
each other and shows different CoD. is strongly correlated with bulk
binding and shows a couple of weaker correlations to other model parameters.
'asurf' is strongly correlated to bulk binding and isovector response
($J,L$). 'asurf symm' is almost exclusively determined by the
isoscalar effective mass and it shows a weak correlation with the
binding energy of $^{236}$U.

Note that the fission isomer energy B(236U) has a large CoD with
isovector response ($J,L$). There was some correlation also for
the Fayans model, but not that large. 

Now we have looked at a standard Skyrme functional SV-min and
an advanced Fayans functional. The latter was fitted to a larger set
of fit data. For completion we also shows for a Fayans functional
fitted to standard data only and with the isovector surface parameter
'asm' included. The fit yields, again, a small value of 'asm', well
compatible with zero within its uncertainty range. However, the
uncertainty of 'asm' becomes much larger than for the fit to full
Fy(IVP3) data which indicates that the extra radius information
in Fy(IVP3) is strongly limiting 'asm'. 

\bigskip


\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{alignmatrix-Fystd-asm}}

\bigskip

The above figure shows the resulting matrix of CoD, now also with
the bulk properties of the super-exotic nucleus $^{266}$Pb.
The 'asm' is strongly correlated to the two isoscalar surface
parameters 'asp' and 'hgradp'. In fact, as in the Skyrme model,
'asm' is fully determined by the other model parameters. The energies
of $^{236}$U and $^{266}$Pb shows some correlation to surface energies,
but to all of them. The radius and the surface thickness of $^{266}$Pb
show correlation with the isoscalar surface parameters. This is kind
of a surprise as one would have expected a larger CoD with the
isosalar surface parameter.

After all, the search for 'asm'-selective, or 'b2p'-selective,
observables was not yet successful. My be we have it already in the
form a multitude of observables, particularly in the Fy(IVP3) fit, and
these prefer this parameter being zero.


