I quick side-issue:
There was the argument that UNEDF1 gives a better extrapolation to
superheavy elements by virtue of fitting to deformed nuclei. This gain
has a price. To illustrate that, the figure below repeats figure 7
from the UNEDF1 paper.

\bigskip

\centerline{\includegraphics[]{UNEDF1-Fig7.jpg}}

\bigskip

The deformed nuclei fit well, indeed. But all near spherical nuclei
are grossly overbound. The fit has simply shifted the problem from
the deformed nuclei to the spherical ones. The basic problem remains:
The functionals in its present from cannot cover simultaneously
the binding energy of super-heavy deformed nuclei and of near spherical
nuclei. Rotational projection helps toward reducing the problem, but
not yet enough for super-heavy elements. 




